-- title:
--   Order 1-q of the reduced modular unit at level one
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$; fix modular polynomial data $data$ for $q$ (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ vanishing on $(j(\mathfrak q), j(\mathfrak q^{q}))$) satisfying the Kronecker congruence $hKr$, integrality hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ for the Hecke operators $\alpha$, $\beta$ at level $1$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a place specialisation $P$ of this data, and a level-one prolongation pair $R$ for $P$, whose first component $R.R_1$ is a regular prolongation of $A$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(1\cdot q)$ with residue field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFullC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,1$. Let $u$ be the element of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(1\cdot q)$ given by the coefficientwise image over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of $\mathrm{modularUnitSeries}(1\cdot q) = \Delta \cdot \Delta_{(1\cdot q)}^{-1}$, and assume $u$ lies in the valuation subring $R.R_1.\mathrm{integers}$. Then the $R_1$-residue of $u$, viewed as a Laurent series over the residue field of $A$, has order exactly $1 - q$.
--
--   The function whose $\mathfrak q$-expansion is $\Delta(\mathfrak q)/\Delta(\mathfrak q^{q})$ is the standard cuspidal unit on $X_0(q)$, with a pole of order $q-1$ at the cusp; this statement records that its order is unchanged after reduction along the first prolongation of $A$. It is used in the computation of the degree of the residue field extension generated by the inverse of this reduced unit.
